   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "99f94b54",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2.3 - Time-evolution patterns and graph centrality measures"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8456ed79",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "By evolving an NA over a graph, we may collect the dynamics of the states of the nodes, i.e. all $\\vec{s}_{t}$, in a so-called time-evolution pattern (TEP). This is a two-dimensional representation of the NA that is highly reminiscent of a spacetime diagram for a cellular automaton. The difference, however, is that a graph does not impose a regular spatial structure between its nodes. This implies that one needs to make a choice regarding how to order the nodes from left to right in the TEP. A clever choice of this ordering will increase the possibility of discerning patterns hidden within the dynamics of the NA.\n",
    "\n",
    "Several choices present themselves, and all are related to particular properties of the nodes. Graph theory (and graph packages in Python, for that matter) suggests a number of possibilities.\n",
    "\n",
    "See also: https://igraph.org/python/tutorial/0.9.6/analysis.html#vertex-properties. Below is a list of all these methods.\n",
    "\n",
    "See also: https://www.turing.com/kb/graph-centrality-measures\n",
    "\n",
    "Also, see Michiel's interaction log with ChatGPT."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "45a8c08d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### _Node degree_\n",
    "\n",
    "We can of course simply order the nodes from left to right, starting with nodes that have the highest number of neighbours. Because the degree $d \\in \\mathbb{N}$, the set of all possible degrees in the network is typically not very large. This means that the ordering of these nodes is typically not fully determined by inspecting the node degrees."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "7315b8d9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "Text(0.5, 1.0, 'Node degree distribution for $N=25$')"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 3,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 400x300 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# for example: node degree\n",
    "N=25; p=.2\n",
    "G = ig.Graph.Erdos_Renyi(n=N, p=p)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(1,1,figsize=(4,3))\n",
    "\n",
    "degrees = G.degree()\n",
    "min_degree = min(degrees); max_degree = max(degrees)\n",
    "bins = np.arange(min_degree, max_degree+2)-.5\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.hist(degrees, bins=bins)\n",
    "ax.set_xlim(min_degree-1.5, max_degree+1.5)\n",
    "ax.set_xticks(bins[:-1]+.5)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"Node degree\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"Number of nodes\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(f\"Node degree distribution for $N={N}$\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7ce2bc3d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### _PageRank_\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a type of eigenvector centrality, and takes into account not only the importance of a single node, but also the importance of the surrounding nodes.\n",
    "\n",
    "The PageRank values of the nodes in a graph were first defined by Larry Page to quantify the importance of a website. It is mathematically (and recursively) defined as\n",
    "$$\n",
    "PR(x_i) = \\sum_{x_j \\in \\mathcal{N}(x_i)} \\frac{PR(x_j)}{|\\mathcal{N}(x_j)|},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "where $\\mathcal{N}(x_i)$ represents the neighbourhood of node $x_i$, excluding $x_i$ itself. Note that for a fully-connected network consisting of $N$ nodes, this results in a PageRank of $1/N$ for each node.\n",
    "\n",
    "One practical advantage of using PageRank is that it is very likely to assign a unique value to each node, even for graphs that are close to regular. One theoretical advantage is that 'the likelikehood to end up on a webpage' or the 'relevance of a webpage' may be an important indicator for how important this node is for the robustness of our entire NA. In other words, this is a good candidate for ordering our TEP."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "d9ac910a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "Text(0.5, 1.0, 'PageRank distribution for $N=1000$\\nContains 1000 unique values')"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 4,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
