       "<Figure size 500x300 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# example: clustering\n",
    "N=1000\n",
    "ps=[0.49, 0.5, 0.51]\n",
    "Gs = []\n",
    "\n",
    "for p in ps:\n",
    "    G = ig.Graph.Erdos_Renyi(n=N, p=p)\n",
    "    Gs.append(G)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(1,1,figsize=(5,3))\n",
    "bins=20 # None\n",
    "\n",
    "for p, G in zip(ps,Gs):\n",
    "    clustering = G.transitivity_local_undirected()\n",
    "    clustering_unique = len(np.unique(np.array(clustering)))\n",
    "    ax.hist(clustering,bins=bins, label=f\"p = {p}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"Clustering coefficient\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"Number of nodes\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(f\"Clustering coefficient for $N={N}$\\nContains {betweenness_unique} unique values per graph\")\n",
    "plt.legend(ncols=3)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e8f0b3f4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Note that a higher rewiring probability (parameter `p`) generates higher clustering coefficients. Generally, the higher the number of nodes $N$, the more clearly the clustering coefficient distributions will be separated. In addition, note that again all clustering values are unique, which is desired for the ordering of our nodes in the TEP."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "26cc62c9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### _Similarity measures_\n",
    "\n",
    "We can compare a node with all other nodes and give a score which quantifies how 'similar' they are, with some well-defined notion of similarity. This results in a similarity matrix $\\Sigma$ with elements $\\Sigma_{ij}$ for $0 \\leq i,j < N$. The `igraph` package provides three variations:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. _Jaccard similarity_: The Jaccard similarity coefficient of two vertices is the number of their common neighbors divided by the number of vertices that are adjacent to at least one of them.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. _Dice similarity_: The Dice similarity coefficient of two vertices is twice the number of their common neighbors divided by the sum of their degrees. This\n",
    "coefficient is very similar to the Jaccard coefficient, but usually gives higher similarities than its counterpart.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. _Inverse log-weighted similarity_: Each vertex is assigned a weight which is 1 / log(degree). The log-weighted similarity of two vertices is the sum of the weights of their common neighbors.\n",
    "\n",
    "Clearly, these measures generate a _vector_ per node, which is perhaps not ideal for ordering the nodes. But they are briefly investigated below regardless."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "id": "3d834a8b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
